WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.589 align:start position:0%
 
we're joined now by one of the two  congressmen who made a so-called  unauthorized visit to kabul this week  seth moulton he's a democrat and he went  there with one of his republican  colleagues congressman moulton is a  member of the house armed services  committee and served four tours in iraq  with the u.s marines congressman good  morning to you  and thank you for your service on a  morning where we're reminded of what's  at stake thanks tony i want to begin  with your reaction to 13 dead service  members in afghanistan  yesterday i mean it's absolutely  devastating  and and i want i i want people to  understand  what they were doing  because it's one of the most heroic  things i've ever seen in my life  out there at abbey gate i expected to  see marines on one side of the gate and  afghans on another  but that doesn't work  because those marines have to go out in  in literally sift through the sea of  humanity of people so desperate to get  out that they're back there today  despite the bombings just to get out  from the taliban  and so you have young  marines out there physically close  feet from the taliban with their whips  yeah  to put little afghan girls  on their backs  hold  afghan women by their hands in in  and pull them to freedom  the security situation there is bizarre  because you've got the taliban who we  were bombing a month ago now doing the  initial security check  for the airport where our troops are  there was a failure there that's how  this bombing got through can this  continue  i mean it's such a cruel irony that  the terrorist group that harbored the  911 attackers  we now have to work with them  to sort through the people who are  trying to get to freedom but there's no  elegant way though congressman to end a  war is there something that should  clearly there is it could or should have  been done differently yeah there's  something you believe it had to go down  this way no it did not chaotic as it is  and let's be clear this failure is not  the troops on the ground  the failure is with us in washington  because all we had to do was start this  evacuation earlier  and we've been pushing the  administration to do this for months all  we needed to do is start the evacuation  earlier because people were getting  evacuated through afghanistan by just  showing up at the embassy like they do  at any other embassy in the world but it  wasn't going fast enough but congressman  i keep hearing nobody expected the  afghan troops to buckle the way they did  with all the training and all the money  i think from the u.s position and i  don't know you certainly know more than  i do on this  everybody thought that the afghan army  can handle this  no yeah that's true but that's not an  excuse for not starting the evacuation  earlier yeah there's no reason why we  couldn't we couldn't do this and then  not put these  young marines in this position i mean  let me just share first you know i've  talked to these guys out there  you know 18 year old lance corporal says  to me  you know so it's pretty tough  when  you get asked  to separate a family because someone  doesn't qualify because their older  sister  is is 19 and doesn't qualify as a  dependent so then i have to take her  hand and  walk her back  to the taliban  while the rest of her family goes to  america that's what we're asking of  these kids  congressman i want to ask you about the  taliban tony mention them being  responsible for security uh we've  learned that u.s officials in kabul gave  the taliban a list of u.s citizens green  card holders uh senator bob menendez is  saying you can't trust the taliban  senator ben sasse is calling it a kill  list what's your reaction well of course  we can't trust the taliban but this  again is a situation  that decision makers in washington have  put these officials on the ground the  only way we can get these american  citizens through the taliban lines  is to tell them who they are now i don't  know we could have to go into the  details of whether we should have really  been giving names or whether there was  some other way to just assure that they  had the right passports  but that's how desperate the situation  is right now you caused a bit of a  controversy when you went without  authorization why did you do that and  what were you looking for  you know  the week before  i've been trying i've stayed up all  night one night just to get four  families through the lives four  four young families  sending hundreds and hundreds of text  messages between the afghans some of  their other friends and in troops that i  happen to know at the base  saying to the the my kids are crying  they don't have enough water we need to  go home no stay there stay there a  little bit longer i've got someone who  knows where you are i'm testing them  grids and showing the pictures of where  they are outside the basin and at the  end of the night after staying up all  night doing this i got one one  of the four families out  when i saw a picture of them  a heroic journalist  his wife and two daughters about the  same age as mine i said  you know what it's worth it  and if i can get on here you went  without if i can get on a plane you're  thinking if i can get on a plane  [Music]  and save a few more lives  [Music]  then then absolutely i'll do that and by  being there i was able to do that there  were several families that  that we got over  just by being there by being because the  pentagon's perspective is different on  this point uh john kirby the  spokesperson for the pentagon said that  that you and your fellow congressmen  they certainly took time away from what  we had been doing that day when you were  there and they were saving lives you  being there they're saying it it made it  harder it probably took time away from  john kirby but i don't care what people  in washington say  the people on the ground i mean  literally were hugging me  thanking me for coming  because america needs to know  what's going on there and i've been in  that position i was a marine in iraq  when i felt abandoned by congress  because people in washington  were making decisions that  they cost that effect lives that cost  lives  and they didn't know what was going on  in the ground they didn't know  what was happening there and i vowed  that if i ever get to congress  i'm not going to leave the troops behind  you learn something there that the  people in washington don't know several  things several things we've already  shared  for example all these refugees are now  going to to refugee camps where they  don't have enough food and water because  administration lawyers are preventing  donations i learned that as much as as a  veteran in congress i wanted to extend  the august 31st deadline in fact i  thought that being there on the ground  would be the best place to advocate for  that  that  because of this crazy relationship we  have with the taliban we can't afford to  do that so  we learned a lot but the single most  important thing i learned is what  what unbelievable heroes these young  americans are we are also  congressman i've never been more proud  to be an american thank you very much  connor  all right

